===Etymology 2===
From {{m|la|in-||[[into]]}} + {{m|en|damnify||to [[injure]]; to [[wrong]]}}, assimilated to {{m|en|indemn}} and {{m|en||indemnify|secure against loss; compensate, reimburse}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} to [[hurt]], to [[harm]]
#* '''1583''', Thomas Stocker's translation of ''A tragicall historie of the troubles and ciuile warres of the lowe Countries'', i. 63a
#*: He... did not [[believe|belieue]] that his [[Majesty|Maiestie]] by this occasion [[could]]e any way be '''endemnified'''.
#* '''1593''', Thomas Lodge, ''Life & Death of William Long Beard'', E ij
#*: What [[harm]]e the [[Rhodian]]s [[have|haue]] [[done|doone]] thee, that thou so much '''indemnifiest''' them?

